    {
        "user": "U08MQU46D96",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1761868066.739979",
        "client_msg_id": "3c808b39-8e61-4c6e-bb5f-24e649a32e12",
        "text": "I went to composition school in the 90s and had a full load of comtemporary comp, post-tonal theory and computer music (Csound + Common Music were the core tools) so I've spend a lot of time in the world of Asher's talk here. Ferneyhough didn't hook me as much as Xenakis did - he and Ligeti were my big contemporary classical influences from early on.  But i have a healthy variety of musical personas.\n\nThere are so many links and citations this makes me think of sharing, and I'm not sure how many others are interested in this intersection of music / architecture / modeling / systems thinking, but I have gotten sucked into this thread on lines every week or so for months: <https://llllllll.co/t/experimental-music-notation-resources/149>\nI love the variety in how people notate what they hear in their heads, and how they organize and model it in the process.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1761836142.530699",
        "parent_user_id": "UJBAJNFLK",
        "attachments": [
            {
                "from_url": "https://llllllll.co/t/experimental-music-notation-resources/149",
                "service_icon": "https://llllllll.co/uploads/default/optimized/3X/1/2/1250a168c49681b4c186dc79a7192d17061746ee_2_180x180.png",
                "thumb_url": "https://llllllll.co/uploads/default/original/3X/e/b/eb8e4fb9cd77a736801390dbe4aebd4fa53a2c20.jpeg",
                "thumb_width": 800,
                "thumb_height": 600,
                "ts": 1427561005,
                "id": 1,
                "original_url": "https://llllllll.co/t/experimental-music-notation-resources/149",
                "fallback": "lines: Experimental music notation resources",
                "text": "As somebody with one foot in the visual art world and the other in sound world, I have always been interested in the experimental music notation strategies. Aside from the fact that these are often beautiful artifacts, I love thinking about the \u201cutility\u201d of images: how/when image becomes an \u201cinstruction\u201d. I think the new |||||||| forum, with it\u2019s expanded conceptual ambition, ought to have a thread devoted to this aspect of new and experimental music. Let\u2019s share resources, files, images, link...",
                "title": "Experimental music notation resources",
                "title_link": "https://llllllll.co/t/experimental-music-notation-resources/149",
                "service_name": "lines",
                "fields": [
                    {
                        "value": "68 mins :clock2:",
                        "title": "Reading time",
                        "short": true
                    },
                    {
                        "value": "3733 :heart:",
                        "title": "Likes",
                        "short": true
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "Xgtpn",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I went to composition school in the 90s and had a full load of comtemporary comp, post-tonal theory and computer music (Csound + Common Music were the core tools) so I've spend a lot of time in the world of Asher's talk here. Ferneyhough didn't hook me as much as Xenakis did - he and Ligeti were my big contemporary classical influences from early on.  But i have a healthy variety of musical personas.\n\nThere are so many links and citations this makes me think of sharing, and I'm not sure how many others are interested in this intersection of music / architecture / modeling / systems thinking, but I have gotten sucked into this thread on lines every week or so for months: "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https://llllllll.co/t/experimental-music-notation-resources/149"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "\nI love the variety in how people notate what they hear in their heads, and how they organize and model it in the process."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
